.TH ACCROCHE 1 "2026-07-31" "bibliothecaire" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
accroche \- hang the harvested pages side by side for arrangement by hand
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B accroche
[\fIlist\fR|\fIstatus\fR]
.br
.B accroche status
.br
.B accroche list
.br
.\" bashify: norun
.B accroche hang
.br
.B accroche order
.br
.B accroche sheets
[\fB\-\-summon\fR]
.br
.B accroche
[\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B accroche
hangs the pages
.BR glane (1)
harvested, side by side, on one flat surface a human can look at and work
from. To hang a show is to put the works where they can be seen; deciding
what hangs next to what is the other half of that job, and this utility does
not do the other half.
.SH WHAT THIS UTILITY WILL NOT DO
.B It does not arrange the pages,
and this is the whole reason it stops where it stops.
.PP
The work these pages are material for is made by a person moving them
around. That act is the product. A utility that ordered them by length, by
theme, by date, by similarity, or by anything else would not be helping with
the work \- it would have performed the work, badly, and left a human to
ratify it. So:
.TP
\(bu
There is no sort, no group, no rank, and no filter that reorders.
.TP
\(bu
There is no saved layout, no drag\-to\-reorder, no arrangement state of any
kind. Nothing here remembers where a page was put.
.TP
\(bu
The pages hang in the order the archive's catalogue happened to hand them
over. That order is an accident of the archive and carries no judgment,
.B and the hung surface says so on its face,
because a sequence that does not explain itself is read as an editorial one.
.B order
prints the same statement at the terminal.
.PP
None of this is a missing feature and none of it is behind a flag. Asked to
arrange, this utility exits
.B 2
\- the caller is asking the wrong thing of it \- rather than exiting 4, which
would promise the capability later.
.PP
The one packing decision that
.I was
declined is worth recording, because it looks purely cosmetic. The hung
surface has ragged vertical gaps: a page of verse is short, a page of prose is
long, and rows start below the tallest card above them. Packing those gaps
tightly requires a column layout that changes the reading order from
across\-then\-down to down\-then\-across. That is the tooling making an
arrangement decision to look tidier, so it was not made.
.SS Subcommands
.TP
.B list
Print every subcommand and whether tooling backs it. Honours
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B status
Report what is hung right now: how many pages the surface holds, how many
texts were skipped as too short, and whether the surface is older than the
harvest it claims to show. Reads; changes nothing.
.TP
.B hang
Build the surface from the harvest. Reads the manifest only, never the cache,
so a tree whose cache has been discarded still hangs completely.
.TP
.B order
State the order the pages hang in, and what that order does and does not
mean. Reads nothing.
.TP
.B sheets
Render the surface to paper and count the sheets that come out. Paper is the
one arrangement surface that cannot drift into automating the arranging, so
it is a first\-class output rather than a side effect, and the sheet count is
the witness that printing works \- the stylesheet existing is not.
.IP
No mechanism for this exists here yet, and that is the ordinary case rather
than a defect: this page was written before the utility, so it promises what
nothing has performed yet. Run without
.BR \-\-summon ,
.B sheets
exits
.B 3
and prints the summon it would have made \- nothing is spent and the gap is
named. Run with it, an agent performs the render
.I and
leaves behind the mechanism that performs it without an agent next time, at
which point this subcommand costs nothing forever after. The escalation goes
through
.BR basheur (1),
the contract store, and never contacts a model from here.
.B accroche list
reports this subcommand as
.B AGENT
until that happens, and as
.B MECHANIZED
after.
.SH THE DENSITY SWITCH
The hung surface prints at one, two, or four pages to the sheet, chosen on
the surface itself. The switch changes ink and nothing else: it drops the
screen furniture, and it does not touch the order the pages come out in. Four
up is a see\-the\-whole\-spread view at roughly five points, not a reading
view.
.PP
The switch is scaffolding for a question that is still open \- which density
is the right physical form \- and not a settled feature. When that question is
answered the other two densities go away.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine\-readable output. Honoured by
.BR list .
Requested of any other subcommand it exits 4 rather than being dropped,
because a flag silently ignored is a caller lied to.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Results on standard output, commentary suppressed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Usage summary, including the exit vocabulary.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the utility name and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-summon
Permit this run to spend money. Accepted by
.B sheets
alone; on anything else it is a usage error naming the refusal. It grants
permission rather than requesting an act, so a run carrying it that needs no
summon costs the same as a run without it. There is deliberately no short
form:
.B \-s
collides with existing tools and
.B \-S
differs from it by one shift key, which is an unacceptable property for the
only flag that spends real money. Spelling the word out is the
deliberateness.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of flags; everything after is a positional argument.
.SH THE COST BOUNDARY
.B Exactly one subcommand of this utility can spend money:
.BR sheets ,
and only while no mechanism for it exists. Every other subcommand reads files
on this machine and writes one file on this machine, reaching no network at
all \- not a paid service and not a free one.
.B \-\-help
names which, so the question "can this cost me anything?" is answerable
without this page.
.PP
.B \-\-summon
is a grant of permission, never an instruction to spend. Passed on a run that
never reaches
.BR sheets ,
it is refused by name as a usage error rather than accepted and quietly
ignored: a caller who habitually passes the flag must not be able to believe
it did something. Passed once
.B sheets
has been mechanized, it costs nothing and says so.
.PP
The direction of travel is that this boundary disappears. A summon buys the
answer
.I and
the mechanism that makes the next answer free, so
.B sheets
is expected to stop costing anything, permanently, after one use.
.PP
Once mechanized,
.B sheets
spends time and a renderer process on this machine. That is local processor,
not a bill.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong. An unknown subcommand, an unknown flag,
.B \-\-summon
on a subcommand that cannot spend, and asking this utility to arrange the
pages are each one of these.
.TP
.B 3
A summon is needed for what was asked and
.B \-\-summon
was not given. Nothing was done and nothing was spent, and the summon that
would have been made is printed.
.B This is a finding, not an error:
it is the ordinary reply from a contracted action whose mechanism has not
been built yet.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: the tooling to keep this promise does not exist here
.I and
no summon can reach it.
.B \-\-json
asked of a subcommand that does not produce it exits this way, as does
.B sheets
when the contract store is not on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran with valid arguments and could not finish \- there was a
harvest to hang and the surface was not built.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read the harvest \- no manifest, or one it cannot parse.
This is "I cannot see", never "nothing to hang", because an empty surface
reported as a clean run looks exactly like an honest one.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GLANE_ROOT
The tree the harvest lives under, shared with
.BR glane (1).
Defaults to the directory above this utility.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I bin/page92.py
The implementation
.B hang
runs.
.TP
.I pages/manifest.json
The harvest record this reads. The cache is never read.
.TP
.I gallery/index.html
The hung surface.
.SH EXAMPLES
Name the utility:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ accroche --version
accroche (bashified)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask what order the pages hang in, and what it means:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ accroche order
archive catalogue order. An accident of the archive, not a reading order.
It carries no judgment, and nothing here will reorder it: arranging is yours.
.fi
.RE
.PP
List the subcommands and whether tooling backs each:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ accroche list
MECHANIZED    hang
MECHANIZED    list
MECHANIZED    order
AGENT         sheets
MECHANIZED    status
.fi
.RE
.PP
Authorise spending on a subcommand that cannot spend. The flag is refused by
name rather than accepted and ignored:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ accroche --summon
accroche: only sheets can spend, so --summon requests nothing for status
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Ask it to arrange the pages. This is refused as a usage error rather than
deferred as a gap, because deferring it would promise it later:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ accroche sort
accroche: arranging is not this utility's to do; that is the work itself
$ echo $?
2
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR glane (1),
.BR verse (1),
.BR basheur (1),
.BR montage (1),
.BR pr (1),
.BR column (1),
.BR test (1)
.PP
Lineage:
.BR montage (1)
in placing many separate images on one surface without deciding what they
mean;
.BR pr (1)
in paginating for paper and leaving the input order alone;
.BR column (1)
in laying records side by side rather than reordering them;
.BR test (1)
in reserving 0 for success and giving distinct nonzero codes distinct
meanings.
.SH HISTORY
Written 2026\-07\-31, when
.B quatre-vingt-douze
folded into
.BR bibliothecaire .
It is a second verb rather than a subcommand of
.BR glane (1)
because harvesting material and hanging it are different acts on different
days, and the constraint this page exists to hold \- that the arranging is
never automated \- belongs to the hanging, where the temptation to automate
it actually lives.
.PP
The surface it describes was checked by rendering it and looking, twice,
after two separate passes reported it working from the source alone: one
found the paper form fragmenting to a page per sheet whatever the density
said, and the other found the screen render had only ever been read as HTML.
The sheet count and the picture are the witnesses; the stylesheet is not.
